Brute Force
-----------
Idea:
- Use a temporary array to store only unique elements.
- Since the array is sorted, compare each element with the previous one.
- Copy the unique elements back to nums.

Algorithm:
1. Add nums[0] to temp.
2. Traverse from index 1 to n-1.
3. If nums[i] != nums[i-1], add it to temp.
4. Copy temp back to nums.
5. Return temp.size().

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int removeDuplicates(vector<int>& nums) {
        if (nums.empty()) return 0;

        vector<int> temp;
        temp.push_back(nums[0]);

        for (int i = 1; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (nums[i] != nums[i - 1])
                temp.push_back(nums[i]);
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < temp.size(); i++)
            nums[i] = temp[i];

        return temp.size();
    }
};
```

Better Solution
---------------
Idea:
- Use two pointers.
- One pointer (i) tracks the last unique element.
- The other pointer (j) scans the array.
- Whenever a new unique element is found, place it after the last unique element.

Algorithm:
1. If array size is 0 or 1, return its size.
2. Initialize i = 0.
3. Traverse j from 1 to n-1.
4. If nums[j] != nums[i]:
   - i++
   - nums[i] = nums[j]
5. Return i + 1.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int removeDuplicates(vector<int>& nums) {
        if (nums.size() <= 1)
            return nums.size();

        int i = 0;

        for (int j = 1; j < nums.size(); j++) {
            if (nums[j] != nums[i]) {
                i++;
                nums[i] = nums[j];
            }
        }

        return i + 1;
    }
};
```

Optimal Solution
----------------
No intermediate improvement exists. The two-pointer approach above is already the optimal solution.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

Interview Notes
---------------
- Since the array is sorted, all duplicates are adjacent.
- Two pointers are sufficient to overwrite duplicates in-place.
- The first (i + 1) elements always contain the unique sorted elements.
- Edge Cases:
  - Empty array.
  - Single element.
  - All elements are the same.
  - No duplicates.
- Follow-up:
  - Why does this fail for an unsorted array?
    - Because duplicates are no longer adjacent; sorting or a hash set would be required.
- This is a classic in-place array manipulation problem and is frequently asked in interviews.
